.6, "sigma_total": 6.28, "baseline_margin": -1.37, "analyst_adjustment": -0.43, "key_factors": ["R+6 district with Republican incumbent Brad Finstad seeking re-election", "October polls show a dead heat, averaging roughly even with a slight Democratic edge", "A Democratic-tilting national environment offsets much of the district's lean", "Jake Johnson has out-raised Finstad, though markets and most raters still favor the incumbent"], "rationale": "Finstad's incumbency and the district's Republican lean give him a narrow edge, but October surveys show a tie race, Johnson has out-raised him $3.5 million to $2.8 million, and a strongly Democratic national environment keeps this within tossup range; prediction markets price the Republican at roughly a 60 percent chance.", "watch": "The ad war over the spot Johnson calls an AI fake, or further movement on affordability and farm-cost issues, could break the dead heat in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Brad Finstad (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Brad Finstad, the Republican incumbent, defends a southern Minnesota seat that leans Republican by about six points against Democratic challenger Jake Johnson. Most professional raters call the race Lean Republican, though three of ten rate it a Tossup, and prediction markets give Finstad roughly a 60 percent chance of holding the seat.\n\nRecent polling tells a tighter story: October surveys show the two tied or within two points, and Johnson has actually out-raised the incumbent, $3.5 million to $2.8 million. A Democratic-tilting national environment and voter focus on affordability and farm pressures have kept the race competitive despite the district's lean. The contest has grown heated, with disputes over a Finstad ad Johnson calls an AI fake and criticism of both sides' advertising. Finstad's incumbency and the district's partisan lean give him a narrow edge, but this remains one of the closer House races on the map.", "history": [{"date": "